Z||000000||Shiner||03-18-2000||06:51 AM||||So, does anybody on the forum play bass?  No, Monkeyman, NOT the fish.  I mean the electric bass guitar.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||5
Z||000001||Cliff Pennock||03-18-2000||07:16 PM||||I started playing classical piano when I was six and tried different instruments in between, amongst which the electrical bass. I liked the bass but for me there's no instrument like the piano. I play Chopin only - he must have been the one man invented the piano for...||||reg||7
Z||000002||Shiner||03-18-2000||11:49 PM||||Cliff,<BR> I started piano at an early age also.  I think I was right around 5 or 6.  I never really got into it, probably because I didn't like the recital crap, and all those other "standard" ways of teaching.  I can play the guitar pretty well, but my hands are really too big for it (I am a huge SOB, around 6'4" and 250lbs)  The bass is really my ideal instrument, I think.  I was thinking about taking up piano again too, since my on again-off again bass teacher is an incredible piano player.  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||b_b_a_d||03-19-2000||06:28 AM||||Well, if my bass was in one piece again...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><BR>I'll probably just buy a new one since it seems in two years I haven't done anything with it.  I was prepping it for a new look and never finished so right now I have a gutted parts bass...  LOL!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  I always loved the sound of a guitar both bass and electric so I bought a bass when I was 18 y/o and splurged on a electric fender guitar about 6 years ago.  <P>Actually, I'm more of a piano man like Cliff.<BR>Been playing piano/keyboard from an early age.  In the last month, I finished installing all my midi hardware/software on my computer so now I have no more excuse to put off recording and writing the music down.    ||||reg||1
Z||000004||87SC/GT||03-19-2000||11:35 AM||||So this is where all the bass player are! I have played guitar for 18 years and drums for about 2. A couple of my buddies and I get together and jam twice a month but we have been unable to find a bass player. Perhaps I should start asking my fiero club members!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||AL||03-19-2000||04:31 PM||||i've been playin guitar for 24yrs, played the viola for 10yrs, but the guitar is my prefferedinstrument to play.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Richard Parnell||03-20-2000||01:53 PM||||I've played bass for over 30 years.  I have an old candy apple red pre-CBS Fender Precision.  It's not real pretty.  It really needs to be cleaned but I keep it hooked up to an amp in the living room in case I feel the need to play.  I've always played mostly rock and blues, but I like most anything classic, jazz. Usually my only criteria is that the musicians can keep a beat and can tune their instruments.  Not really meaning to be a name dropper, I used to live in a suburb of Tacoma, WA (Lakewood)  there were a number of bands that played around there.  One of the musicians was a friend of mine named Robert Cray.  We all used to get together and jam. He was good even then. That was way before he became famous.||||reg||6
Z||000007||Shiner||03-20-2000||07:38 PM||||Richard,<BR> Hey, I'll trade you a set of side scoops for that pre-CBS P-bass.  Sound like a good deal? lol My main bass is a Fender Foto-flame Pbass, which is one of the ones made in Japan.  I tried $900 american standard Jazz and Pbasses that sounded and played like crap, and finally found this little gem at a show for $400.  Not to brag, but I tried some pretty expensive basses with big names, and I didn't like any of them more than mine.  The only thing I would want to do with it is maybe drop one of those Seymour Duncan Antiquity pickups in it, which are supposed to sound exactly DEAD ON like an old pre-cbs pickup.  <P> That Robert Cray thing is pretty damn cool too!!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Richard Parnell||03-20-2000||07:55 PM||||Tempting but sorry no deal.  I've priced the bass when I've gotten down (like in the blues) and it's worth more than my little Fiero. My son who is a Fender Strat  guitar player would die if I sold that bass.  <BR>Yeah knowing Robert is cool.  I haven't talked to him in years.  I remember when he and his original band decided to go to Chicago (this was in the early '70s) we all thought they were nuts.  We told them they were going to die in that city.  Years passed and one night while watching the tonight show, they intro'd a hot new blues player.  It was Robert, I almost fell off the couch.  I saw him recently at a show in Waikiki and tried to get back stage to see him but with all the women rushing the stage it was a no go.  I'll have to try an arrange it with the promoter next year.  It will be fun, he's one of the nicest guys you could ever meet.||||reg||6
